    "chunk_index": 80,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "54250fd824d2e86bf30e69d41fcd9443",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "The Disaster Management Centre - DMC - relies on official forecasts and technical assessments issued by the Department of Meteorology and other competent scientific institutions. The DMC continuously monitors hazard information and undertakes preparedness coordination based on the official forecasts received.\n\n2067 2068\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db8\u0dda\u0da2\u0dbb\u0dca \u0da2\u0dd9\u0db1\u0dbb\u0dcf\u0dbd\u0dca (\u0dc0\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb\u0dcf\u0dc1\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb\u0dcf\u0db8\u0dd2\u0d9a) \u0d85\u0dbb\u0dd4\u0dab \u0da2\u0dba\u0dc3\u0dda\u0d9a\u0dbb \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf]\u0dbb \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf]\n\nDepartment of Meteorology\n\nA strong El Ni\u00f1o event is currently developing and is expected to intensify further during August-October, 2026.\n\nThe World Meteorological Organization - WMO - and leading global climate centres indicate that El Ni\u00f1o conditions will continue through the end of 2026, with a 97 per cent probability of persisting into early 2027. A positive Indian Ocean Dipole - IOD - is also forecasted to occur simultaneously, which may enhance climate impacts across the Indian Ocean Region.\n\nThe combined influence of El Ni\u00f1o and the positive IOD or the Indian Ocean Dipole may increase the risk of floods, particularly during the latter part of the year.\n\nEl Ni\u00f1o is one of the strongest drivers of global climate variability and can significantly influence rainfall and temperature patterns.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, an above-average rainfall is expected during October, November and December, 2026.\n\nThe Department of Meteorology continually monitors these developments and provides regular monthly and weekly weather forecasts to support national disaster preparedness and decision-making.\n\nThe Government has been closely monitoring the evolving El Ni\u00f1o and positive IOD conditions, strengthening preparedness measures for possible extreme weather events and utilizes the forecasts issued by the Department of Meteorology for timely planning and risk management."
  },
