    "md5": "203a59c0f79aa6041d88c010b20e1cde",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1480,
    "chunk_text": "If we consider all of this - the reforms we started, the reforms that are currently being implemented in the system, as a whole, starting with making sure that the Police and the CID are able to carry out proper and\n\nOur Hon. Minister listed out many of the improvements we have made to the system, including making the system more efficient through filling vacancies in it, increasing the number of opportunities for hearings to be held, focusing on jurisdictions where there is a heavy caseload and making sure that the justice system is supported through technology and additional support staff. So, all of these changes have been made to the system. Also, special initiatives have been put in place for courts which have heavy caseloads. Recruitments have to be made to the Government Analyst's Department, which has been neglected for many years, which has been one of the major causes for the delays in the investigation processes and where vacancies having been piling up over the years. All of these have been rectified or in the process of being rectified.\n\nWith this Amendment, we are able to, as required, establish Appeal Courts in the provinces. There are various interpretations being brought about with regard to these Amendments we have initiated or what is being made possible, but the fact of the matter is that each of these Amendments and each of these initiatives made through this are an attempt to make justice more accessible to all without discrimination."
