    "chunk_size_bytes": 1719,
    "chunk_text": "Under the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution Bill and the Judicature (Amendment) Bill, no judge receives a personal extension at the pleasure of the President or any other Member of the Government. There is no application form, no political favours or Executive discretion. The rule is same for everyone; from the Magistrate's Courts to the Supreme Court, it is a general application of one rule. That is precisely why the Supreme Court rejected the argument that the Amendment amounts to a personal inducement. That was addressed in the Special Determination on the Twenty Second Amendment to the Constitution Bill and the Judicature (Amendment) Bill, Sir. The Court said that the proposed Amendment applies to all judges and is not a special benefit requested by or granted to a particular judge. Addressing the issue of conflict of interest, it had been distinguished between the direct personal interest in a specific case and a general institutional interest arising from the legislation, affecting the Judiciary. Therefore, this is not about one judge or one case, this is about all the judges across the spectrum.\n\nThen, when it comes to the question of judicial independence, Sir, which has been raised over and over again during the last few months, we must discuss it honestly. Judicial independence is not protected merely by repeating the phrase or holding up a poster or a placard, but by clear laws, a secure tenure, transparent appointments, adequate resources and respect for court decisions. Sri Lanka has painful examples of what happens when those safeguards fail. We do not have to look far back. In 2013, the then Chief Justice Shirani\n\n2107 2108\n\n2026 \u0dc3\u0dd0\u0db4\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd0\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 24"
