  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-24-2026-09-24",
    "num": "2026-09-24",
    "date_str": "2026-09-24",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-09-24",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24400_english_2026-09-24.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-24-2026-09-24-0158",
    "chunk_index": 158,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "dc641b79b64ca828c6da6a06a5acc865",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1700,
    "chunk_text": "can and should make that change or amendment. However, if we leave it to the Executive, that is an entirely different matter, Sir. Then, that will be discretionary, which the Twenty Second Amendment does not seek to do.\n\nSir, the Twenty Second Amendment must, therefore, be understood in its proper context: it is a structural measure, applies equally to all judges, removes individual Executive discretion, strengthens judicial capacity, retains institutional experience, introduces a term limit for future Chief Justices and places a question before the constitutional forum authorized to decide it.\n\nSir, we should definitely learn from the past, but should not be imprisoned by it, especially when we exercise the sovereign power of the people. The Impeachment of Shirani Bandaranayake showed the danger of political interference; the treatment of a successor showed the danger of changing governments treating judicial office as a political prize. The answer is not to leave the law frozen in place; the answer is to establish clear, general and predictable rules that apply to everyone. No judge should hold office because a politician prefers him. No judge should leave office because a politician dislikes the judgment given by him. No judge should depend on the Executive generosity for continued service. That is the principle behind this Amendment. The Court of Appeal and the High Court are being strengthened for the public. The Office of the Chief Justice is being protected from indefinite concentration of power by introducing a cap, Sir. Therefore, the Government asks Parliament to judge this Amendment not by suspicion or speculation, but by its text, the structure and its effect."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
