    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "dcfc5ef6adb3668a365863e723132566",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "They could have easily cornered the Members of the Opposition by having a consultative process, bringing in the civil society in a big way, but they chose not to do that. They chose not to do that in a background where this Government had never, never, stated the increase in the retirement age of the judges as one of their pledges during\n\nThe other matter I wish to raise, Sir, is the question of independence of the Judiciary. The retirement ages of the Superior Court Judges are entrenched in the Constitution. It is done so because when they are appointed, it brings certainty with regard to their term, and that is very important. When you have certainty, then everybody knows that that judge, whichever way he swings, whatever his ideas may be and whatever his judgments may be, would be there for a certain term. So, if the idea was, \"Look, we need to overhaul these; the ages that have been stipulated are far too low\", they must be increased. They must be increased, in principle, I am in agreement. If the new ages were to be applied to the new appointments, nobody could complain. Then, that would be a totally different attitude towards the Constitutional change. In fact, that would be something which would be welcomed because new members are new members and they are not noted individuals. The moment you are appointed, you are a noted individual; your name is noted with that position. The consequences, therefore, when you try to change their ages thereafter is, you stand the risk of being accused, as you are today, of political interference. And, that sort of accusation, then, brings the whole question of independence of the Judiciary into question.\n\nUnfortunately, they have been making that case, that this is just a bunch of rogues seated in the Opposition,\n\n2197 2198\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0db8\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\u0dd4\u0dc0"
